%N A399700 Array read by diagonals. Consider a wheel constructed from a circle of diameter 2n+1 units, surrounded by a rim. Cross the circle with horizontal bars, each of thickness 1 unit, and each at 1 unit from the next. Superimpose another identical wheel. The n-th row of the array, referring to wheels of n bars, maps the variation over time of the number of resulting holes as the second wheel rotates.
%C A399700 See link for details.
%C A399700 It appears that row n has period length A007590(n+1). - _Pontus von Brömssen_, Sep 10 2026
%H A399700 John Mason, <a href="/A399700/a399700.pdf">Counting holes in superimposed circular grids</a>
%e A399700 Two wheels with 1 bar each produce 2 holes, then 4, then 2, etc., so the first line is: 2 4 2 4 2 4.
%e A399700 Example rows. Only the first values are shown, with the second wheel rotating from 0 to 90 degrees.
%e A399700   1: 2 4
%e A399700   2: 3 7 9
%e A399700   3: 4 6 10 14 12
%e A399700   4: 5 9 13 15 19 23 21
%e A399700   5: 6 8 12 16 20 24 26 30 28 32
%e A399700 As the second wheel rotates beyond 90 degrees, each row reflects and repeats the previous values:
%e A399700   1: 2 4  2  4  2  4  2  4  2  4  2  4  2  4  2  4  2  4  2  4  2  4  2  4  2  4  2  4
%e A399700   2: 3 7  9  7  3  7  9  7  3  7  9  7  3  7  9  7  3  7  9  7  3  7  9  7  3  7  9  7
%e A399700   3: 4 6 10 14 12 14 10  6  4  6 10 14 12 14 10  6  4  6 10 14 12 14 10  6  4  6 10 14
%e A399700   4: 5 9 13 15 19 23 21 23 19 15 13  9  5  9 13 15 19 23 21 23 19 15 13  9  5  9 13 15
%e A399700   5: 6 8 12 16 20 24 26 30 28 32 28 30 26 24 20 16 12  8  6  8 12 16 20 24 26 30 28 32
